# YouTube RAG Assistant — Implementation Plan

## 1. What This Project Is

A web app where a user pastes one or more YouTube video links, multiple links, or a playlist link. The system fetches transcripts, indexes them for semantic search, and lets the user ask natural-language questions about the content. Answers are generated from the actual transcript (RAG, not the LLM's general knowledge), and every answer comes with clickable source cards that jump the embedded player to the exact timestamp where that topic was discussed — across whichever of the ingested videos actually cover it.

**One-line resume pitch:** "Built a multi-video RAG system that ingests YouTube playlists, retrieves timestamp-grounded answers across sources, and deploys via a single config-driven codebase to both a local dev profile and a rate-limited public demo on Azure."

## 2. Core Features

- Accept single video links, multiple links, or a playlist link in one input (mixed input parsed and deduplicated).
- Hard cap of 5 videos per collection (config-controlled — disabled in local dev profile).
- Transcript ingestion via YouTube's own captions (no paid ASR).
- Sentence-aware chunking that preserves original timestamps.
- Semantic search scoped to the user's own collection (not global).
- LLM-synthesized detailed answer, generated only from retrieved transcript chunks.
- Ranked source list under each answer: video thumbnail, title, timestamp, short excerpt — grouped so the same video isn't listed twice.
- Single embedded YouTube player that loads the correct video and seeks to the correct timestamp when a source card is clicked.
- Session-based isolation with no login: a session ID scopes all data, so users never see each other's collections.
- Sliding-expiry auto-cleanup: inactive sessions (and everything tied to them) are deleted automatically.
- Ingestion progress visibility (so a 5-video batch doesn't look frozen while processing).

## 3. Tech Stack

| Layer | Choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Backend | FastAPI (Python) | async-friendly, fast to build REST APIs |
| Video resolution | yt-dlp (metadata only) | resolves playlists/videos to IDs without downloading audio |
| Transcript source | `youtube-transcript-api` | free, fast, uses YouTube's own captions |
| Embeddings | Gemini embedding API | separate free quota from chat generation |
| Answer generation | Gemini (Flash model) | free tier, sufficient for low-traffic demo |
| Vector storage | PostgreSQL + pgvector | one database for metadata + vectors, supported both locally and on Azure |
| Background jobs | FastAPI `BackgroundTasks` + Postgres `jobs` table | see note below — no Redis/Celery needed at this scale |
| Frontend | React (or Next.js) | source cards + embedded YouTube IFrame Player API |
| Local deployment | Docker Compose | one-command clone-and-run |
| Hosted deployment | Azure Container Apps (consumption plan) | scales to zero when idle, protects the $100 student credit |

**Correction from earlier planning:** Celery/Redis was floated earlier for handling playlist-scale ingestion. That was written before the 5-video hard cap was decided. At a max of 5 videos and ~2-3 users a week, a real job queue is unnecessary infrastructure to build, run, and pay for. FastAPI's built-in `BackgroundTasks`, combined with a `jobs` status table in Postgres for progress tracking, is sufficient. Revisit only if the cap or traffic assumptions change.

